{"subjectId":"sql","id":"select-basics","title":"SELECT Basics","group":"Querying Basics","order":11,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>SELECT</code> reads data: pick columns, compute expressions, and shape the result set. Every other query feature builds on this.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-tree\"></i>Anatomy of a SELECT</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT   column_list        -- what to return   (required)\nFROM     table_name         -- where from\nWHERE    row_filter         -- which rows\nGROUP BY grouping_columns   -- collapse rows into groups\nHAVING   group_filter       -- which groups\nORDER BY sort_keys          -- in what order\nLIMIT    n;                 -- how many</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Only <code>SELECT</code> is mandatory — but the clauses must appear in exactly this order.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-columns\"></i>Choosing columns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, salary, hire_date\nFROM employees\nORDER BY emp_id\nLIMIT 4;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary | hire_date\n------+--------+-----------\nAsha  | 150000 | 2015-03-01\nRavi  | 120000 | 2017-06-15\nMeera | 95000  | 2019-01-10\nKaran | 95000  | 2020-08-01</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calculator\"></i>Computed columns and literals</h3>\n<p>The select list can contain any expression: arithmetic, function calls, string concatenation (<code>||</code>), constants.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name,\n       salary,\n       salary / 12            AS monthly,\n       salary * 12 / 100      AS pf_12_pct,\n       'INR'                  AS currency,\n       upper(name) || ' #' || emp_id AS badge\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id = 1\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary | monthly | pf_12_pct | currency | badge\n------+--------+---------+-----------+----------+---------\nAsha  | 150000 | 12500   | 18000     | INR      | ASHA #1\nRavi  | 120000 | 10000   | 14400     | INR      | RAVI #2\nMeera | 95000  | 7916    | 11400     | INR      | MEERA #3\nKaran | 95000  | 7916    | 11400     | INR      | KARAN #4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Integer ÷ integer is <strong>integer division</strong> in SQLite, PostgreSQL and SQL Server: <code>7 / 2</code> is <code>3</code>. Use <code>7 / 2.0</code> or <code>CAST(x AS REAL)</code> for a fractional result. (MySQL and Oracle return <code>3.5</code>.)</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math\"></i>SELECT without FROM</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT 2 + 3 * 4 AS result, 7 / 2 AS int_div, 7 / 2.0 AS real_div, 'hi' || '!' AS greeting;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>result | int_div | real_div | greeting\n-------+---------+----------+---------\n14     | 3       | 3.5      | hi!</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Oracle</div><p>Oracle requires a <code>FROM</code>, so it provides a one-row dummy table: <code>SELECT 2 + 3 FROM DUAL;</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-asterisk\"></i>SELECT *</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Name the columns you need: less data over the network, can use covering indexes, stable when columns are added.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p><code>SELECT *</code> in production code or in views — it returns columns you don't need and changes shape silently when the table changes.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is SQL case-sensitive?</summary><div><p>Keywords and (unquoted) identifiers are case-insensitive in all major engines. <strong>String comparison</strong> depends on the collation: <code>'a' = 'A'</code> is false in PostgreSQL and SQLite (binary), true in MySQL and SQL Server with their default case-insensitive collations. Quoted identifiers (<code>\"Name\"</code>) are case-sensitive in PostgreSQL and Oracle.</p></div></details></section>"}